  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Global Facility for Disaster Risk Reduction and Recovery (GFDRR) 2026-2031</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">GFDRR supports communities and institutions to prepare for, withstand, and recover from shocks caused by natural hazards and climate events. GFDRR works with countries to mainstream disaster risk management (DRM) and climate adaptation into development planning, and to embed disaster and climate resilience in policies, systems, and investments.  GFDRR provides grants, technical assistance, and shapes partnerships to strengthen resilience at local, national, and regional levels. Such action supports countries to implement their commitments under the Sendai Framework for Disaster Risk Reduction (SFDRR), the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development, and the Paris Agreement, including efforts to address Loss and Damage associated with the impacts of climate change. 

GFDRR is structured around the 4 elements of the Sendai framework:
- understanding disaster risk (DR)
- strengthening DR-informed policies, plans and regulations
- managing DR by investing in DR reduction measures 
- enhancing disaster preparedness, response and recovery capacity

GFDRR is a global partnership, and multi-donor umbrella trust fund, managed and hosted by the World Bank&apos;s Infrastructure Vice Presidency. GFDRR&apos;s 12 donors, leverage the World Bank&#x2019;s financial, technical, and policy expertise to build countries&apos; disaster and climate resilience. World Bank teams, channel GFDRR&apos;s grant finance to support countries&#x2019; projects and operations, thus amplifying the impact of the Worldbank&apos;s development finance.  

GFDRR&apos;s scope is global (ODA recipients). But in the policy dialogue, individual contributors focus on different regions. The Netherlands will focus on priority regions in West Africa, Horn of Africa and MENA, and on countries where it has bilateral water cooperation, on combi-countries where NL has an economic interest and on countries that NL has categorized as of interest from a perspective of migration dialogue or hosting of refugees.  

GFDRR is managed by the Infrastructure Vice-Presidency. It focuses on cities and densely populated coastal zones, as hot spots for disaster risk. This aligns with Dutch policy focus on economic development and migration, as cities are hubs for economic growth (over 80% of economic growth happens in cities), and are also poles on migration routes (rural to urban; small town to large city; country to country). Moreover, given the high rate of urbanization in The Netherlands, and its coastal location, the Dutch have specific expertise on urban and coastal resilience. GFDRR could serve as a vehicle to showcase Dutch expertise and create market opportunities for export of advisory services, and for contractors.</narrative>
  </description>
